    "math_academy_id": "q-73250",
    "knowledge_point_id": "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/a8a70a2f28585b63d2a083b42eb6ad54bfb581b5f13c699a3bc3c8d836afaee8.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The function is continuous at $x=1$\n\nII. The function is continuous at $x=0$\n\nIII. The function is differentiable at $x=0$",
    "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
